# Everything under scripts/pbx runs on the Linux PBX, and the installer carries
# an embedded byte-for-byte copy of vitalpbx-inbound-route-helper.py guarded by
# a byte-identity test (install-vitalpbx-inbound-route-helper.test.ts).
#
# On Windows, core.autocrlf=true checks the .sh out as CRLF while the .py stays
# LF, so that guard can never pass locally — it fails by construction, gets
# ignored, and the embedded helper silently drifts. It drifted twice: to
# 2026.07.26.1 (missing apply_tenant_changes entirely) and again to 2026.08.06.2
# while the live PBX ran 2026.08.06.6, where running the installer would have
# DOWNGRADED the PBX and wiped the doorway-hijack fix.
#
# Deliberately scoped to scripts/pbx rather than a repo-wide *.sh rule: 113 shell
# scripts elsewhere are CRLF in working trees and a blanket flip would churn
# unrelated in-flight work.
/scripts/pbx/** text eol=lf

# docs/pbx carries reference copies of scripts and Asterisk .conf that get
# scp'd to the Linux PBX (media-sync, prompt-sync, voicemail-drop context).
# Same CRLF trap as above, same scoped fix — .sh and .conf only, so the .md
# files keep normal Windows behavior. Added 2026-08-12 when docs/ stopped
# being gitignored wholesale.
/docs/pbx/*.sh text eol=lf
/docs/pbx/*.conf text eol=lf
